; FILED: `modulo` with an inexact operand and an INTEGRAL result loses its
; inexactness in the VM — the VM is the wrong side here.
;
; R7RS 6.2.1: "if any argument is inexact, then the result is also inexact".
; The two substrates print the same digits, so the corpus differential (which
; compares printed output) cannot see this at all — only `exact?` separates
; them, which is why this is filed rather than added to corpus/50.
;
;                              native -r   vm
;   (exact? (modulo 5.0 3))    #f          #t   <- wrong
;   (exact? (modulo 5   2.0))  #f          #t   <- wrong
;   (exact? (remainder 5.0 3)) #f          #f
;   (exact? (modulo 5.5 2.0))  #f          #f
;   (modulo 5.0 3)             2           2    (values agree)
;
; `remainder` keeps its inexactness and so does a non-integral `modulo`, so
; this is specifically the VM collapsing an INTEGRAL flonum result to an exact
; integer — the VAL_INT collapse family in the VM's numeric tag dispatch, not
; a floored-remainder bug. Native's flonum `modulo`/`remainder` paths were
; themselves wrong until the fix that added corpus/50; with native correct,
; this became visible.
(display (exact? (modulo 5.0 3)))
(newline)
(display (exact? (modulo 5 2.0)))
(newline)
(display (exact? (remainder 5.0 3)))
(newline)
(display (exact? (modulo 5.5 2.0)))
(newline)
